  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of member States to ratify and apply international labour standards and to fulfil their reporting obligations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In close collaboration with the Government of Papua New Guinea (GoPNG), the ILO is undertaking discussion for supporting a strategic programme of work in 2025 focused on advancing comprehensive labour law reform. This effort responds to GoPNG&#x2019;s identified priorities, which include strengthening legal protections against discrimination, ensuring equal remuneration for work of equal value, prohibiting sexual harassment in the workplace, eliminating child labour, and safeguarding workers against unfair dismissal.
These reform areas are critical to promoting decent work and are well aligned with the observations of the ILO Committee of Experts on the Application of Conventions and Recommendations (CEACR). Building on this alignment, the ILO is encouraging renewed consideration of the 2013 draft Employment Relations and Industrial Relations Bills. These drafts offer a foundational basis to address longstanding gaps in PNG&#x2019;s labour legislation and bring national laws closer into conformity with international labour standards.
In addition to legislative reform, the programme identifies several key policy and procedural challenges that require focused attention. These include the need to improve access to labour justice mechanisms, ensure inclusive stakeholder consultation processes, and establish institutional arrangements that promote transparency and accountability. By addressing these issues in a coherent and consultative manner, PNG will be better positioned to implement reforms that are not only legally robust but also broadly supported by government, employers, and workers.
The ILO remains committed to supporting the Government and social partners through technical assistance, policy dialogue, and capacity building to achieve these outcomes and contribute to sustainable and inclusive development in Papua New Guinea.</narrative>
  </description>
